@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "NULL Functions";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            SQL Server NULL Functions
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            NULL Functions in SQL Server are used to handle missing,
            unknown or NULL values in database columns.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is NULL?
        </h2>

        <p>
            In SQL Server, <strong>NULL</strong> represents a missing,
            unknown or unavailable value.
        </p>

        <p>
            NULL is not the same as zero, an empty string or a blank
            space. It means that no value is available.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">

            <strong>Example:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                Suppose an employee has not provided a phone number.
                The PhoneNumber column may contain NULL because the
                value is not available.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         NULL FUNCTIONS TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common NULL Functions
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Function</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>ISNULL()</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Replaces a NULL value with a specified value.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>ISNULL(Phone, 'Not Available')</code>
                        </td>

                    </tr>


                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>COALESCE()</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the first non-NULL value from
                            a list of expressions.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>COALESCE(Phone, Email, 'N/A')</code>
                        </td>

                    </tr>


                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>NULLIF()</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Returns NULL when two expressions are equal.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>NULLIF(10, 10)</code>
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         1. ISNULL()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            1. ISNULL() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>ISNULL()</strong> function is used to replace
            a NULL value with a specified replacement value.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>ISNULL(expression, replacement_value)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Simple Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT ISNULL(NULL, 'Not Available');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

            <strong>Output:</strong>

            <br />

            Not Available

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example with Column
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    ISNULL(PhoneNumber, 'Not Available') AS PhoneNumber
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-3">
            If PhoneNumber contains NULL, SQL Server displays
            <strong>Not Available</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         2. COALESCE()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            2. COALESCE() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>COALESCE()</strong> function returns the
            first non-NULL value from a list of expressions.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>COALESCE(expression1, expression2, expression3, ...)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    COALESCE(NULL, NULL, 'Hello');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

            <strong>Output:</strong>

            <br />

            Hello

        </div>

        <p>
            SQL Server checks the expressions from left to right
            and returns the first non-NULL value.
        </p>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Practical Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    COALESCE(PhoneNumber, Email, 'No Contact Available')
        AS ContactInformation
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-3">
            If PhoneNumber is NULL, SQL Server checks Email.
            If Email is also NULL, it returns
            <strong>No Contact Available</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         3. NULLIF()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            3. NULLIF() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>NULLIF()</strong> function returns NULL
            when two expressions are equal.
        </p>

        <p>
            If the two expressions are different, the first
            expression is returned.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>NULLIF(expression1, expression2)</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example 1: Values are Equal
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT NULLIF(10, 10);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">

            <strong>Output:</strong>

            <br />

            NULL

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example 2: Values are Different
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT NULLIF(10, 20);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

            <strong>Output:</strong>

            <br />

            10

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         NULL COMPARISON
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            How to Check for NULL Values?
        </h2>

        <p>
            We should use <strong>IS NULL</strong> and
            <strong>IS NOT NULL</strong> to check NULL values.
        </p>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            IS NULL
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Employees
WHERE PhoneNumber IS NULL;</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query returns employees whose PhoneNumber is NULL.
        </p>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            IS NOT NULL
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Employees
WHERE PhoneNumber IS NOT NULL;</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query returns employees whose PhoneNumber
            contains a value.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            NULL vs Zero vs Empty String
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Value</th>
                        <th>Meaning</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>NULL</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Value is missing or unknown.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>0</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Numeric value zero.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>''</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Empty string.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>' '</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            String containing a space.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example with Employees
        </h2>

        <h5 class="fw-bold">
            Create Table
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Employees
(
    EmployeeId INT,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(100),
    PhoneNumber VARCHAR(20),
    Email VARCHAR(100)
);</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Insert Data
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Employees
VALUES
(1, 'Rahul', '9876543210', 'rahul@gmail.com'),
(2, 'Priya', NULL, 'priya@gmail.com'),
(3, 'Amit', NULL, NULL);</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Display Replacement for NULL
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    ISNULL(PhoneNumber, 'Phone Not Available') AS PhoneNumber
FROM Employees;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Use COALESCE()
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    COALESCE(
        PhoneNumber,
        Email,
        'No Contact Available'
    ) AS Contact
FROM Employees;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Find Employees Without Phone Number
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Employees
WHERE PhoneNumber IS NULL;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         ISNULL VS COALESCE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            ISNULL() vs COALESCE()
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>ISNULL()</th>
                        <th>COALESCE()</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            Accepts two arguments.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Can accept multiple expressions.
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            SQL Server-specific function.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Standard SQL expression.
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <code>ISNULL(Phone, 'N/A')</code>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>COALESCE(Phone, Email, 'N/A')</code>
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHEN TO USE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            When Do We Use NULL Functions?
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To handle missing database values.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To replace NULL values with meaningful values.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To display user-friendly reports.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To select the first available value from multiple columns.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To check whether a column contains NULL.
            </li>

            <li>
                To safely handle missing data during calculations.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning">

            <h4 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NULL means the value is missing or unknown.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NULL is different from zero.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    NULL is also different from an empty string.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use IS NULL to find NULL values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use IS NOT NULL to find values that are not NULL.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ISNULL() can replace a NULL value.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    COALESCE() returns the first non-NULL value.
                </li>

                <li>
                    NULLIF() returns NULL when two expressions are equal.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-primary">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                NULL handling is an important part of SQL Server
                development. In this topic, we learned how NULL
                values work and how to use ISNULL(), COALESCE(),
                NULLIF(), IS NULL and IS NOT NULL. These techniques
                are useful when working with incomplete or missing
                database information.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>

